Check for no contacts and flush the tunnels with the SPI list locked.

ims_registrar_pcscf's save_pending() calls ipsec_reconfig() before it inserts
a new contact. When usrloc holds no contacts, ipsec_reconfig() rebuilds the
SPI list (clean_spi_list()) and then flushes every SA and policy in the
kernel (ipsec_cleanall()). Only an atomic flag guards this, and it only
stops a second caller from running the cleanup too. Nothing stops another
process from installing a tunnel while the cleanup runs.

That happens whenever two UEs send their first REGISTER at once to a P-CSCF
with no contacts:

  worker A (UE 1)                  worker B (UE 2)
  no contacts, sets the flag
  clean_spi_list() (holds the      "reconfig already called"
    SPI lock; slow with a large    inserts its contact, relays the
    ipsec_spi_id_range)              REGISTER, gets the 401
                                   acquire_spi() waits for the lock
  releases the SPI lock            acquires a pair, installs its SAs
  ipsec_cleanall() flushes them      and policies
                                   relays the 401

UE 2 then sends its protected REGISTER over SAs that are gone, and the
kernel drops it without a trace in the SIP log. In the logs, the flush shows
up as fewer "already installed" answers for that tunnel's redundant SAs and
policies (3 instead of 8 when port_uc == port_us).

Run the check and the whole cleanup with the SPI list locked. A tunnel is
installed with a pair acquired after its contact was inserted. So either
that contact is counted and nothing is flushed, or acquire_spi() waits until
the flush is over. The flag has nothing left to guard and goes away. The
lock order is unchanged: callers already take a usrloc slot lock and then
the SPI lock, and the cleanup takes no usrloc lock.

